# Coach speech (Italian): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Italian needs.
# Register: the coach says "tu" to the player (the game menus say "tu" too, as Italian software
# does). The coach is male ("sono contento", "sono pronto"). The player's gender is unknown: nothing
# may agree with the player (no "bravo", "sei pronto", "sei stato", "ti sei arreso"): use avere,
# nouns or impersonal phrasing ("complimenti", "ben fatto", "hai giocato bene", "ce l’hai fatta",
# "hai abbandonato").
# Terms (FSI usage): mossa, casa, colonna, traversa, scacco, scacco matto, stallo, patta, arrocco
# corto / lungo, en passant, promozione, forchetta, inchiodatura, infilata, attacco di scoperta,
# errore grave / errore / imprecisione.
# Typography: the typographic apostrophe ’ (l’alfiere), È with its accent, no space before ! ? : ;
# quotes « ».

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns and genders (FSI usage; the queen is "donna", never "regina"): il pedone, il cavallo,
# l’alfiere, la torre, la donna, il re. piece.<type>.gender is m, f, or mv = a masculine noun that
# starts with a vowel (alfiere), whose article elides: l’alfiere, dell’alfiere, all’alfiere. Only the
# patterns that put an article right before the noun (white, black) need an ".mv" variant; every
# other pattern falls back to the masculine one ("il tuo alfiere").
#
# A piece placeholder always carries its owner: {your} {your2} = the player's ("il tuo cavallo"),
# {my} {my2} = the coach's ("il mio alfiere"), {t1} {t2} = either; in the Analysis mode {piece} {t1}
# {t2} carry a side instead ("il cavallo bianco", "la torre nera"). Never write an article, a
# possessive, or di / a / da / in / su (they fuse with the article: del, al, dal, nel, sul) right
# before a placeholder: ask for its form. A preposition that does not fuse (con, per, tra, fra,
# contro, verso, senza, dietro, dopo, oltre, sopra, sotto) goes before the plain form ("per {your}").
# The coach points at the piece on its placeholder: every line names the piece with a full form
# ({your}, {t1:a}, {my:art}, {your:poss}...); the pronoun and ending forms (lo, gli, lui, o) never
# replace it and come after it (except an ending right before the noun: quest{your:o} {your:poss}).
# Every form below is defined for all six pieces and the owners your, my, white and black.
#
# Forms with the owner (your / my: article + possessive + noun; white / black: article + noun +
# colour adjective):
#   nom     il tuo cavallo, la tua torre, il mio alfiere / l’alfiere bianco, la donna nera
#           (subject, object, after a preposition that does not fuse)
#           "{my} attacca {t1} e {t2}."  "Prendo {your}."
#   di      del tuo cavallo, della tua torre / dell’alfiere bianco, della torre nera
#           "{your2} era l’unico difensore {your:di}."
#   a       al tuo cavallo, alla tua torre / all’alfiere bianco, alla torre nera
#           "{your} è inchiodat{your:o} {t1:a}."
#   da      dal tuo cavallo, dalla tua torre / dall’alfiere bianco, dalla torre nera
#           "{your} è attaccat{your:o} {my:da}."
#   in      nel tuo cavallo, nella tua torre / nell’alfiere bianco, nella torre nera
#           "Ho fiducia {my:in}."
#   su      sul tuo cavallo, sulla tua torre / sull’alfiere bianco, sulla torre nera
#           "Forchetta {t1:su} e {t2:su}!"
#   con     con il tuo cavallo, con la tua torre / con l’alfiere bianco (the same as "con {your}")
#           "Prendi {my} {your:con}!"
#   poss    tuo cavallo, tua torre / alfiere bianco, torre nera (no article: after a demonstrative)
#           "Guarda quest{your:o} {your:poss}." = "Guarda questo tuo cavallo." / "questa tua torre."
# Forms without the owner (the same words for every owner; use them when the owner is obvious):
#   art     il cavallo, la torre, l’alfiere                "Muovi {your:art}!"
#   art_di  del cavallo, della torre, dell’alfiere         "Pensa alla sicurezza {your:art_di}."
#   art_a   al cavallo, alla torre, all’alfiere            "Pensa {your:art_a}."
#   art_da  dal cavallo, dalla torre, dall’alfiere         "Allontana il re {my:art_da}."
#   art_in  nel cavallo, nella torre, nell’alfiere         "Ora tutta la forza è {my:art_in}."
#   art_su  sul cavallo, sulla torre, sull’alfiere         "La pressione cresce {your:art_su}."
#   un      un cavallo, una torre, un alfiere              "Ho perso {my:un}, non la partita."
#   bare    cavallo, torre, alfiere (the noun alone)       "{your:bare} inchiodat{your:o}." (terse
#           lines: "Cavallo inchiodato." / "Torre inchiodata.")
# Pronouns and endings that agree with the piece (the same for every owner):
#   lo      lo / la, the direct object pronoun: "Prendi{my:lo}!" = "Prendilo!" / "Prendila!",
#           "Ora {your:lo} attacco." Before a vowel or the h of avere write "l’" yourself (both
#           genders) and let the participle agree: "l’ho pres{my:o}".
#   gli     gli / le, the indirect object pronoun: "{your} è in trappola: non {your:gli} resta
#           nessuna casa."
#   lui     lui / lei, the stressed pronoun after a preposition: "{t2} è proprio dietro di {t1:lui}."
#   o       o / a, the ending of a word in -o / -a that agrees with the piece, joined to its stem:
#           "{your} è inchiodat{your:o}" = "inchiodato" / "inchiodata"; also quest{t1:o},
#           l’altr{t1:o}, "te l{your:o} prendo" (te lo / te la), gliel{my:o}. Words in -e (forte,
#           debole, vulnerabile) and "in presa" do not change.
